1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
This Java library has a full JUnit 5 suite
and 95% line coverage — green across the board.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:20,000
But run PIT mutation testing instead: it flips a conditional,
changes a boundary, removes a return value, and checks if any test fails.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:35,000
82% mutation score. Roughly one in five logic mutations
slips through completely undetected.

4
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Line coverage can't see that gap. Mutation score can.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
With Java, Maven and Python installed,
every round is one command: sh run_round.sh

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
It compiles the project, runs PIT, and writes two files:
survivors.md and a new row in rounds.csv.

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Three survivors: MoneyUtils line 26 misses the exact boundary value.

8
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Validators line 128 — an overload no test ever calls.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,000
SimpleCache line 142 — the expiry branch never runs.

10
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:40,000
One focused test per survivor: a boundary assertion,
a direct overload call, a time-advance test for the cache.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:55,000
The score climbs, survivors.md gets shorter.
Repeat: read, write a test, run.

12
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:15,000
chart.py plots this from rounds.csv.
Blue = line coverage (95→99%). Green = mutation score (82→95.8%).

13
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Coverage was already "done". Mutation score wasn't.

14
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Before → After: Line coverage 95%→99% · Mutation score 82%→95.8% · Survivors 48→11

15
00:02:40,000 --> 00:03:00,000
37 of 48 previously-surviving mutants are now caught.
One survivor, one test, one command.
